
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">&#8220;It seems that philosophers have started to make substantial use of AI in their papers, and it’s not uncommon for AI-generated text to make it into philosophy published in decent journals.&#8221;</p>



<span id="more-27581"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Not uncommon. How much are we talking here?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That&#8217;s the question <a href="https://maxnoichl.eu/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Maximillian Noichl</a> (Utrecht) recently took up. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">His answer? An estimated &#8220;12% of published philosophy in 2026 [involves] some AI assistance,&#8221; though he notes that the figure will vary depending on the AI detection software used and how the false-positive threshold is set.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So AI writing in philosophy articles is not yet normal. But, he notes, it is increasing. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the following guest post, Noichl explains how he investigated the issue.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">(A version of this post <a href="https://maxnoichl.eu/blog/2026/ai-in-philosophy-publishing/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">first appeared at Noichl&#8217;s site</a>.)</p>



